{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"No. Historical exchange share volume is only a partial—and sometimes misleading—measure of a US ETF’s liquidity. It reflects trading in ETF shares on exchange, not the full liquidity available through the underlying portfolio or off-exchange trading.\n\nAuthorized participants (APs) can create ETF shares by delivering a basket of securities (or cash) to the fund, and redeem shares by receiving the basket (or cash) in return. This arbitrage mechanism helps align the ETF’s market price with its net asset value and can expand or contract shares outstanding. It does not guarantee that a particular order can be executed at a given price or size.\n\nAlso examine:\n- **Bid–ask spread and quoted depth**, including at different times of day and for relevant order sizes.\n- **Underlying holdings’ liquidity**: trading volume, spreads, market depth, and any restrictions or market closures.\n- **Premium/discount to NAV** and intraday indicative values, interpreted cautiously.\n- **Typical and stressed-period spreads, volume, and price behavior**; market-maker presence and the ETF’s creation/redemption basket.\n\nThese are observable clues, not guarantees. Liquidity can change quickly; use limit orders and consider a broker or liquidity provider for a large or complex trade."}}
